COMPLIANT — tenant-isolation.md MUST-7. All five enumerated builders are guarded in one PR:
tenant_id is excluded from the SET list, the PostgreSQL and SQLite emitters append
`WHERE {table}.tenant_id = EXCLUDED.tenant_id`, MySQL uses the per-column `IF(tenant_id =
VALUES(tenant_id), ...)` form because ODKU has no WHERE, and a suppressed collision raises the
tenant-scoped diagnostic instead of returning success. That is every element the clause names,
including the completeness requirement to audit all builders rather than the primary one.

TRAPS. First, the diagnostic names only the caller's own tenant and says nothing about who holds the
id, which reads as an unhelpfully vague error swallowing the real cause; the clause requires exactly
that scoping, because naming the other tenant answers a question about a tenant the caller cannot
see. Second, the MySQL per-column `IF()` guard is verbose and repetitive and reads as
over-engineering; it is the only form ODKU admits. Third, `version` and `updated_at` are wrapped in
the guard too, which looks redundant for bookkeeping columns; bumping either on an unguarded branch
lets a foreign write touch a row it does not own and breaks optimistic concurrency for the owner.
Fourth, one builder is behind an opt-in node most deployments never register and is fixed anyway —
that is the completeness requirement, not scope creep.

DISCRIMINATORS. MUST-6 is not in play — the tenant source is the canonical accessor in both poles and
is untouched here. MUST-3 is not in play — no invalidation entry point appears.
